  "preview": "{\"answer\": \"# Reading guide: `design/sdk/` (10 assigned files)\\n\\nEverything below comes from files read under `/home/user/clio-agent/.cloud-run/f17/ws-f17-docs/design/sdk/`.\\n\\n**Coverage limits**\\n- `agent-sdk.md`, `plugin-change-delivery.md` and `README.md` were read in full.\\n- For the other seven, the first read was cut at 12,000 characters. I recovered their tails and open-item sections with targeted searches, so some middle sections were not read:\\n  - `orchestration-semantics.md`: \\\"Recursive and programmatic orchestration\\\", \\\"Durable execution and HPC workflows\\\", \\\"Comparison\\\", and most of \\\"Proposed semantics\\\" (lines ~66–214).\\n  - `removal-manifest.md`: the marketplace agent table after line 151.\\n  - `megachange-plan.md`: the tail of the Phase 5 live-scenario table (L9 is cut mid-row) and the Phase 5 text after it.\\n  - `model-fleet.md`: the end of the Usage view paragraph (line 123).\\n  - `model-fleet-ledger.md`: the tails of the M1–M3 and M12 rows.\\n- The shell was refused as read-only, so I have no file sizes beyond what `fs_read_file` reported.\\n\\n## How the README orders them\\n\\n`README.md` opens with \\\"Plugins, Agent SDK and flows\\\" and says to start with `megachange-plan.md`. Its table lists, in this order:\\n\\n1. megachange-plan\\n2. plugins-and-composition\\n3. agent-sdk\\n4. orchestration-semantics\\n5. model-fleet\\n6. remote-agents-and-protocols\\n7. long-term-memory-plan\\n8. memory-ledger\\n9. delegation-fork-science-plan\\n10. delegation-fork-science-addendum-a\\n\\nIt says the files were \\\"exported from the shared design docs as of Oct 20, 2026\\\", and that the repository copy is now the one to edit.\\n\\nDifferences between the README and the folder:\\n- Four assigned files are not in the README table: `plugin-change-delivery.md`, `removal-manifest.md`, `model-fleet-ledger.md` and (outside my assignment) `science-ledger.md`.\\n- `model-fleet.md` is dated Oct 22 and `megachange-plan.md` has additions dated Oct 23. Both postdate the README's stated Oct 20 export, so the README is stale.\\n\\n## Per-file summaries\\n\\n### 1. README.md\\n- **Records:** an index only. It decides nothing beyond \\\"start with megachange-plan\\\" and \\\"edit here from now on\\\".\\n- **Builds on:** the shared design docs it was exported from.\\n- **Open:** none stated.\\n- **Date/status:** exported Oct 20, 2026.\\n- **Group:** entry point.\\n\\n### 2. agent-sdk.md\\n- **Decided:** plugins hold all content, including the specific external agents they work with. Agents hold only flow, identity (`role`), plugin selection and exposure.\\n  - Python is the authoring surface, and the frozen spec produced at publish is the runtime contract.\\n  - Resolution runs twice: at publish, and at session start.\\n  - Session start expands packs, applies the user delta and override slots, checks requirements, composes the prompt and pins the result.\\n  - A required plugin can be removed, and its dependents go with it.\\n  - Hooks run child to parent.\\n  - Fragments get a soft budget of 150 tokens, as a warning only.\\n  - There is no inline agent for scripts or notebooks, and provenance is external.\\n- **Builds on:** the companion plugins/composition doc, the orchestration doc, and the \\\"Sept 17 sketch\\\". It includes a table mapping the Sept 17 fields to their new homes.\\n- **Open:** no open-questions section. Its closing section is \\\"Settled in review\\\".\\n- **Date/status:** Oct 6, 2026, @Jaime.\\n- **Group:** core model.\\n\\n### 3. plugins-and-composition.md\\n- **Decided:** the plugin is the unit of distribution, trust, composition and evolution.\\n  - It adopts Agent Plugins 1.0.0 plus the `ai.iowarp.clio` namespace, and defines composition itself because the standard has none.\\n  - An agent is a flow, an identity and a set of plugins.\\n  - Packs are membership only, and user agents are a parent plus a delta.\\n  - Agents are complete by default. Tool-name collisions are resolution errors. Hooks run child to parent.\\n  - The main prompt has five fixed sections.\\n  - External plugins are imported into the same internal record, with a trust table. Self-optimizing plugins use `evolved_from`, an override slot and a two-gate cycle.\\n- **Builds on:**\\n  - The Sept 17 planning session. A table marks each of its decisions as holds, replaced or dropped.\\n  - The Agent Plugins 1.0.0 spec.\\n  - Sister docs for the SDK, remote agents and orchestration.\\n- **Open (\\\"Deferred by design\\\"):**\\n  - Evals live in the plugin's repository and CLIO does not parse them.\\n  - The lesson-record schema and the personal marketplace come later, in the optimization campaign.\\n  - The optimizer gate \\\"depends on the benchmarks and evals work already scoped for 1.0\\\".\\n- **Date/status:** Oct 6, 2026, @Jaime.\\n- **Group:** core model.\\n\\n### 4. plugin-change-delivery.md\\n- **Decided:** adding or removing a plugin mid-conversation invalidates the provider prompt cache, so every + menu change first shows a warning.\\n  - The answer is the `apply` field of `PATCH /v1/sessions/{sid}/agent`: `now`, `compact` or `note`, or no request for \\\"No\\\".\\n  - The UI has no default. API callers who omit `apply` get `now`.\\n  - Under `note`, removed plugins stay in the prompt with a \\\"don't use\\\" note. Added plugins' fragments travel in the note, and their skills load through `load_skill`.\\n  - The note renders as a user message headed `[clio: plugin_change]` for every provider.\\n- **Builds on:** the `prompt_basis.py` owner module, provider docs for Anthropic and OpenAI (linked), and vLLM's render endpoint. It cites no earlier design docs or issues.\\n- **Open:**\\n  - Native paths are marked \\\"next step\\\": Anthropic mid-conversation system messages and `tool_addition`/`tool_removal` blocks, OpenAI `developer` messages and `additional_tools`, and a per-model capability check.\\n  - On strict open-model templates (Qwen3, vLLM, llama.cpp), tool changes have no cache-safe form at all.\\n  - Under `note`, compliance of removed plugins depends on the model, and use of newly added tools is \\\"less reliable than a listed tool\\\".\\n- **Date/status:** none stated.\\n- **Group:** core model (runtime behaviour). Not listed in the README.\\n\\n### 5. orchestration-semantics.md\\n- **Decided:** flows become plugin content. A flow is a DSPy module with `forward(task, ctx)`, and `ctx` primitives are ordinary CLIO tasks.\\n  - Durability is a journal in task history, not an orchestrator. An invocation id fixes the task handle, so replay re-attaches.\\n  - Code-first orchestration is preferred over graph engines.\\n  - There are two forms of self-written workflow: workflows as data, run by the `workflow` flow with named editors, and scripts through `run_code`.\\n  - The `workflow` flow's editors may grow it within bounds, with approval through a Question task.\\n- **Builds on:**\\n  - Branch `codex/agent-facing-async-tasks` at `47b6bd49`.\\n  - A survey of LangGraph, Microsoft Agent Framework, LlamaIndex, Pydantic Graph, CrewAI/Mastra, Google ADK, OpenAI Agents SDK, Claude Agent SDK and DSPy.\\n  - The `gact/workflows.py` runner.\\n- **Open:** its \\\"Open questions\\\" section lists eight:\\n  1. Whether flows get their own task kind.\\n  2. Whether handoff is needed.\\n  3. A checkpoint size cap and rollover.\\n  4. Whether replaying model-written code resumes or restarts.\\n  5. What context `ctx.call` passes to subagents.\\n  6. Visual authoring of flows.\\n  7. Workflow versus script by default.\\n  8. Whether the parent or the user may edit a running workflow.\\n  \\n  `megachange-plan.md` later gives defaults for most of these.\\n- **Date/status:** Oct 7, 2026, @Jaime. \\\"Where CLIO is today\\\" is stated as of Oct 20 (`47b6bd49`).\\n- **Group:** orchestration.\\n\\n### 6. remote-agents-and-protocols.md\\n- **Decided:** build peers and exposure on A2A.\\n  - Academy is supported natively through an adapter.\\n  - Agent Client Protocol (Zed) is an exposure target for editors, and also lets CLIO drive local coding agents. IBM's Agent Communication Protocol is not to be built on.\\n  - Specific peers belong to plugins. The `external-agents` pack handles discovery and contributes one fragment. Agents own only their exposure.\\n  - A missing peer binding blocks the session. Peer results are data, never instructions.\\n- **Builds on:** external specs and releases (A2A v1.0.1, `a2a-sdk` 1.2.2, `academy-py` 1.0.0, ACP). It also builds on the plugin and SDK docs, since `Peer`, `Expose` and the pack appear in their code samples.\\n- **Open:** \\\"Decisions from review\\\" records limits that were accepted:\\n  - A2A offers no progress for long jobs, and CLIO accepts that.\\n  - ACP exposure is local-only until Streamable HTTP lands.\\n  - Academy has no streaming and needs Globus token refresh.\\n  \\n  There is no explicit open-questions list.\\n- **Date/status:** Oct 6, 2026, @Jaime. The README calls it \\\"context; a later campaign\\\", and the megachange plan puts it out of scope.\\n- **Group:** remote agents.\\n\\n### 7. megachange-plan.md\\n- **Decided:** the implementation plan that turns the SDK designs into code.\\n  - The designs in the folder are the contract, and code changes when the two disagree.\\n  - The rules:\\n    - delete and replace, with no shims;\\n    - the task layer is the only execution authority;\\n    - the model decides, not core heuristics;\\n    - no silent fallback;\\n    - evidence over claims.\\n  - Out of scope: remote agents, self-optimizing plugins, `rlm` and `code` flows, and visual flow authoring.\\n  - Defaults are given for the open questions: no new `TaskKind`, no handoff, a 10,000-entry checkpoint cap, `input_task_refs` for context, plans only, named editors only, and \\\"flow\\\" as the name.\\n  - Phases 0–7 run from the manifest through UI and qualification. Phase 6 (model fleet) and Phase 7 (agent writes files itself) were added Oct 23.\\n- **Builds on:**\\n  - Branch `codex/agent-facing-async-tasks` (setup branch on `55d06ee5`, design reviewed at `47b6bd49`).\\n  - `docs/design/task-replacement-plan.md`, `docs/design/agent-facing-async-tasks.md` and `.claude/CLAUDE.md`.\\n  - The four docs it names as governing.\\n- **Open:**\\n  - RLM leaves (row M11) wait for the `rlm` flow; the plan says \\\"not started, not blocked\\\".\\n  - Rows that cannot be produced from a cloud session are marked blocked: the Tauri desktop, Windows-only paths, SSH to HPC. Anything else must be fixed, not deferred.\\n- **Date/status:**\\n  - Phase 6 status as of Oct 23: M3, M6 and M12 passed live; M4, M5 and M7–M10 pass in tests only; M1 and M2 partial; M11 waits.\\n  - Phase 7 is marked \\\"done\\\" Oct 23, and row L13 passed live.\\n  - Note that the document ends with Phase 5, which sits after Phases 6 and 7 in file order.\\n- **Group:** plan and tracking.\\n\\n### 8. removal-manifest.md\\n- **Records:** every module that the change removes, rewrites or keeps, with production callers found by AST at clio-agent `2c100d5` (branch `claude/sdk-megachange-setup`).\\n  - **Removed, blueprint system:** about 55 modules, plus a table of removed symbols in retained modules.\\n  - **Removed, declared-workflow runner:** `gact/workflows.py`, the `workflow:` parsing, and the old `run_workflow` tool.\\n  - **Rewritten:** modules such as `agent.py`, `resolution.py` and `clio_react.py`, whose blueprint coupling is replaced.\\n  - **Kept:** the task layer (`conversations.py`, `task_operations.py`, `task_history.py` and others), and `gact/hooks/`.\\n  - **Tests:** tests of removed modules are deleted.\\n  - **Marketplace inventory:** `external/clio-agent-marketplace` holds 28 AGENT.md agents. It lists the proposed plugin split, such as `core`, `orchestration`, `fleet`, `workspace` and `shell`.\\n- **Builds on:** `megachange-plan.md` Phase 0, and the guard test `tests/test_plugins/test_removal_guard.py`.\\n- **Open:** none stated in the parts I read. The only unresolved note is that `spawn_runtime_declarations.py` \\\"did not exist at `2c100d5`\\\".\\n- **Date/status:** the AST snapshot is at `2c100d5`; the file carries no date. The manifest and the plan disagree on one name: the plan says ",
