full text of #4, #5 and #7, as described under each entry.\\n- The cited issues (#948, #916, #1057, #1079–#1082 and others), which I did not look up.\", \"error_info\": null, \"expert_handoffs\": [], \"response_parts\": [{\"agent_id\": \"explore\", \"id\": \"part_18d2f3ddc300\", \"metadata\": {\"signature_field_name\": \"next_thought\"}, \"text\": \"<invoke_note>I'll start by listing the folder and file sizes.</invoke_note>\", \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"agent_id\": \"explore\", \"id\": \"part_5ad0d01160ef\", \"metadata\": {\"signature_field_name\": \"next_thought\"}, \"text\": \"Shell is blocked because this session is read-only, so I'll read the files with fs_read_file.\", \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"agent_id\": \"explore\", \"id\": \"part_8cbd13b82ab7\", \"metadata\": {\"signature_field_name\": \"next_thought\"}, \"text\": \"Four files were truncated in the inline view, so I'll pull their headings first, then read the relevant sections.\", \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"agent_id\": \"explore\", \"id\": \"part_c29f6dba32e6\", \"metadata\": {\"signature_field_name\": \"next_thought\"}, \"text\": \"Several files were cut off in the inline view, so I'm reading the missing sections directly.\", \"type\": \"text\"}, {\"agent_id\": \"explore\", \"id\": \"part_69439027175a\", \"metadata\": {\"signature_field_name\": \"answer\"}, \"text\": \"# Reading guide: seven design docs in `design/`\\n\\n**Coverage caveat.** `shell_bash` was refused because this session is read-only, so I read the files with `fs_read_file` and `fs_search_text`.\\n- **Read in full:** `goal-command-presentation.md`, `independent-goal-controls-2026-10.md`, `loop-goal-cron-research-2026-07.md`.\\n- **Read in part:** the other four files were cut off in the inline view, and I did not read their full spilled copies. Their summaries rest on what I saw: the opening, the full heading list, and the sections quoted below. Anything not listed under \\\"Read\\\" for a file is not covered by its summary.\\n\\nPaths are all under `/home/user/clio-agent/.cloud-run/f17/ws-f17-docs/design/`.\\n\\n## Group A: Goal / loop / schedule (current behaviour and its research)\\n\\n### 1. `goal-command-presentation.md` (1,408 B)\\n- **Decided:** `/goal` records a service notice with `source: goal` and an explicit result code (`goal_started`, `goal_stopped`, `goal_rejected`, `goal_command`). Start and stop receipts keep the objective. The conversation shows a Goal entry with a target icon and an \\\"Open Work\\\" action. That action only navigates; it does not re-run the command, restart work or stop a goal. Goal receipts get no \\\"Done\\\" footer. Older text receipts are projected from their recorded metadata, and ordinary prose mentioning `/goal` is not reclassified. The Work finder and footer use the same Goal icon, kept distinct from loops, schedules and todos, and the footer drops the duplicate todo count at narrow widths.\\n- **Builds on:** it cites no earlier docs or issues. It refers only to the existing notice block and Work view.\\n- **Open:** none stated.\\n- **Status:** no date or status line.\\n- **Topic group:** goal UI/presentation. Read after #2.\\n\\n### 2. `independent-goal-controls-2026-10.md` (3,830 B)\\n- **Decided:** the goal controller pursues an observable objective, not an iteration counter or timer. Setting a goal starts work at once. Each completed turn gets an independent completion check, and an unmet objective continues. Explicit human limits take precedence, and omitted limits are unlimited.\\n  - **Judge:** it sees bounded recent evidence plus a retained progress ledger and cannot run tools. A failed judge pauses the goal, a decision needing human input parks it, and ten identical final responses stop continuation.\\n  - **Loops and schedules** are independent of goals: goal completion never cancels monitoring, and a stopped loop never stops the goal.\\n  - **Harness injections:** judgments, loop wakes, scheduled wakes and task completions are recorded as typed harness injections with algorithm authorship, not as human prompts.\\n  - **`wait_for`** parks a goal on durable task handles. `all` waits for every task and `any` resumes after the first. `wait_tasks` stays the in-turn blocking tool.\\n  - **Restart recovery:** retained goals inspect task outcomes and workspace state before replanning. A pending loop wake remains, and an in-flight loop stops explicitly.\\n  - **Fleet:** judges for goals, variants/refinement and permission risk are chosen independently. Permission review classifies none/low/medium/high, and high or unavailable reviews escalate to the user.\\n- **Builds on:** it cites only external research: Magentic-One, Anthropic's long-running harness guidance and LangGraph persistence. It cites no CLIO issue numbers.\\n- **Open:** no explicit open items. Stated limits: \\\"completion is judged evidence, not proof of scientific correctness\\\", and restart recovery \\\"is not replay or rollback of arbitrary external operations\\\".\\n- **Status:** 2026-10 in the filename; no status line.\\n- **Topic group:** goal/loop/schedule (current behaviour).\\n- **Supersession (my reading, not stated in either file):**\\n  - This doc reads as the later state of `loop-goal-cron-research-2026-07.md`. The older doc says \\\"a judge-met goal stops any armed loop\\\"; this one says goal completion never cancels monitoring.\\n  - The older doc makes typed `max_iters`/`max_tokens`/`max_wallclock` bounds the hard stops; this one says omitted limits are unlimited, with human limits taking precedence.\\n  - Neither file declares the relationship, so verify against the issues or code.\\n\\n### 3. `loop-goal-cron-research-2026-07.md` (9,045 B)\\n- **Decided:**\\n  - **Goal evaluation (A4 #1057):** an LLM judge only, with the typed loop bounds as the hard stops. The earlier hybrid with a deterministic `StatePredicate` gate was deleted, because that state is written by the acting model, so a predicate over it lets the model \\\"mark its own homework\\\".\\n  - **Goal is not model-callable:** only a read-only `goal_status()` exists, with no `set_goal` tool.\\n  - **Recommended design:** three doors per capability (command, tool, skill-effect), with the command and skill catalogs kept separate.\\n    - Loop: `/loop`, a `loop_wakeup` tool, and cancel-both.\\n    - Goal: `/goal`.\\n    - Cron: `/cron` and a `cron_create/list/delete` triad. This is an extension of the existing `scheduler.py`, not a rebuild.\\n  - **Universal safety properties:** server-generated IDs, explicit `stop`/`recurring` booleans, a list-back tool, interval and lifetime clamps, and failure-only notification.\\n- **Builds on:** governance-surfaces campaign #1057, Pillar 4 (#1079 loop, #1080 goal, #1081 cron, #1082 skill-autonomy effects), A4 #1057, and the P1.0 skill effects. It follows plan mode and hooks as the earlier pillars. It draws on external surveys: Claude Code, Codex, Gemini, Devin, LangGraph, Temporal and others.\\n- **Open / risks stated:**\\n  - **Accepted residual:** the judge reads the transcript, so it is persuadable by transcript content. This is bounded, not eliminated.\\n  - **Cron gaps:** single fixed action, no retry or backoff, no `max_fires`/`run_at`, no timezone, no cron ranges, HTTP-only.\\n  - **Hard live gate:** Windows-cron.\\n  - **Pitfalls listed:** unbounded self-reschedule, cancel-foreground not cancelling the background schedule, which a daemon is more exposed to, and recursive fan-out in a loop.\\n  - The per-capability designs live in issues #1079–#1082, not in this doc.\\n- **Status:** dated 2026-07-28; a research survey prepared for the campaign.\\n- **Topic group:** goal/loop/schedule (research). Read before #2.\\n\\n## Group B: Governance surfaces research (plan mode, hooks)\\n\\n### 4. `planning-mode-research-2026-07.md` (71,710 B)\\n- **Read:** the TL;DR, the heading outline, §7.1 and §7.5–7.7, §8, and the start of §9. §§1–6, the rest of §7 and most of §9 I did not read in detail.\\n- **Decided / recommended:**\\n  - **Four separable components:** a permission mode `plan`, a re-injected prompt, a markdown file artifact, and a zero-parameter `plan_exit` tool with N-way approval.\\n  - **Enforcement:** a declarative deny-by-default policy with annotation-based re-allow, not prompt-only enforcement.\\n  - **Read-only line:** repo-tracked mutation, not any write.\\n  - **TODO tool** is separate from plan mode.\\n  - **Approval choices:** auto, interactive, exit-only, clear-context, or reject with feedback.\\n- **Builds on:** it is a survey of Claude Code, Codex, Gemini CLI, OpenCode, Cline, Roo, Aider, CUGA, Magentic-One, Manus and others. It cites Claude Code issues, e.g. #13638, #33037 and #60329. The prepared-for line names clio-agent campaign item #4.\\n- **Open:** §7.7 lists optional design spikes: stall-triggered replanning, an epistemic ledger, budget-aware planning, operator playbooks, and save-and-reuse.\\n  - §7.6 says several TODO-tool choices must be made explicitly: one `in_progress` vs several, whole-list replacement, and blocked vs completed.\\n  - §8 lists failure modes F1–F12. F12, a prompt injection that leaves plan mode, is \\\"not observed; structurally possible everywhere\\\".\\n- **Status:** dated 2026-07-24; survey plus recommendation.\\n- **Topic group:** governance surfaces (what the agent may do).\\n- **Relationships:** the hooks doc cross-references it (its open question 4 on where the plan-mode carve-out lives). The loop/goal/cron doc is the next pillar after it.\\n\\n### 5. `hooks-research-2026-07.md` (51,133 B)\\n- **Read:** §0–1, §2.1–2.3, parts of §4.2–4.4, §5.1, §6.7 and §7. The rest I did not read.\\n- **Decided / recommended:**\\n  - **Wire protocol:** adopt the convergent subprocess protocol. Stdin JSON, exit 0 means proceed, exit 2 means block, and anything else is a non-blocking error.\\n  - **Architecture:** one internal onion-middleware interface with subprocess, HTTP and in-process adapters, and a 12-event set cut at three layers.\\n  - **Invariants:** hooks may only tighten policy, never loosen it, and a hook failure must be distinguishable from a user rejection.\\n  - **Format:** adopt Amp's tagged-union contract.\\n  - **Config schema:** §5.4.\\n  - **Invariants and tests:** six invariant",
